  <!-- Use paragraphs to break apart subsections -->
    <paragraphs>
      <title>What to call me</title>
      <p> You may address me as
      “Dr. Austin” or “Professor Austin”, and I prefer the pronouns
      “he” and “him”.  Please be respectful of your instructors and
      learn how they wish to be addressed.  “Professor” is more
      appropriate than, say, “Mr.” or “Mrs.” but just ask if you ever
      feel uncertain.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Office Hours</title>
      <p> Office hours are times that I am guaranteed to be available
      to you, and you are encouraged to stop by.  Sometimes students
      want to talk about something in the course that is challenging
      or intriguing to them.  But other times, students want to talk
      about a wide variety of other things: resources they can take
      advantage of, career opportunities, some current event, or what
      made me want to be a math professor at Grand Valley.  This is
      time for you, and I’m happy to talk with you about whatever is
      on your mind.
      </p>
      <p>
        It’s hard for me to find a time to hold office hours when all
        my students are available.  I’m flexible and can arrange to
        meet with you at another time, if that works better for you.
        We could also meet by Zoom, if you’re not on campus.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>AI Policy</title>
      <p>This is complicated, and we'll talk about it in class, but
      please don’t use generative AI to complete any assignments or to
      check your work in this course.  As full disclosure, I will tell
      you that I use AI a lot in a software project I'm involved in.
      This task is very different from actually learning mathematics,
      which is your job in this class.  You may think:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            “I will be using AI in my career.”  With the current state
            of AI, you won’t be using it to learn new ways of
            thinking, like we’re trying to do in our course.  AI will not
            help you develop an understanding of mathematics.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><q>I only use it to check my work when I don’t feel totally
          confident.</q>  I am part of a math education research study
          that investigates how students learn math from textbooks.
          Interviews with students clearly show how using AI causes
          students to become less confident over time since they come
          to trust what comes from AI more than they trust their own
          work.  Learning will be challenging and facing challenges
          requires you to have the confidence that you can do it.
          Please don’t surrender the trust that you have in your own
          abilities.
          </p>

        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is one research study, among many others:
        <url href="https://www.media.mit.edu/publications/your-brain-on-chatgpt/">
          <em>Your Brain on ChatGPT</em>.
        </url>
        From the abstract:
        <blockquote>
          <em>
            Over four months, LLM users consistently underperformed at
            neural, linguistic, and behavioral levels. These results
            raise concerns about the long-term educational
            implications of LLM reliance and underscore the need for
            deeper inquiry into AI's role in learning.
          </em>
        </blockquote>
      </p>

      <p>
        There's also this from Nick Deanes, who graduated from GVSU
        with a math degree in 2016 and serves as
        Global Director of
        Talent Management at JR Automation, a company many of you
        would be thrilled to work for.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <em>
            Majors and job titles change; the ability to think
            clearly, solve problems, communicate, and adapt
            doesn't. Investing in these areas will always keep
            most opportunity doors open for you.
          </em>
        </p>
      </blockquote>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Phone policy</title>
      <p>
        Inside our classroom, please keep your phone stored with
        your belongings and away from your work space, unless you have
        an exceptional situation such as concern for a friend or
        family member.  This includes time when you are waiting for
        class to begin as this provides everyone with the opportunity
        to develop productive working relationships.
      </p>

      <p>
        We live in an age where your attention is
        being monetized by others, and it is not readily apparent the
        price that you are paying.  An overwhelming amount of
        research, however, plainly shows how disruptive this
        technology is to your learning.
        <url href="https://www.nytimes.com/2018/10/26/style/phones-children-silicon-valley.html?unlocked_article_code=1.dk8.6ygB.2TOc7QaMEx3N=url-share">
          “On the scale between candy
          and crack cocaine, it’s closer to crack cocaine,” Mr. Anderson
          [former editor of Wired magazine] said of screens.
        </url>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Technology</title>
      <p>If you have a laptop/tablet/Chromebook, please bring it to
      class.  We will use free, online computational tools, such as
      Desmos, which is superior to a traditional calculator. If you
      already have a calculator, you are welcome to use it, but you
      don’t need to buy a calculator for this course.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Communication</title>
      <p>I will be posting assignments, activities, and other relevant
      information on our course Blackboard page. I will also be
      communicating with you frequently through email. Please make
      sure that you check your Grand Valley email on a daily basis.
      Please don’t send messages to me through Blackboard’s messaging
      system because I am unlikely to see them in a timely fashion.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Late work</title>
      <p>
        While assignments will always have due dates, I recognize that
        life happens and that you don’t owe me an explanation when it
        does.  Throughout the semester, you are allowed to submit an
        assignment late on three occasions provided you write to me
        beforehand and let me know when you will complete it.  Please
        note that this policy does not apply to learning target
        quizzes.  If you are habitually having a hard time handing
        things in on time, please come and talk with me.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <!-- Other subsections for larger dividers -->
  <subsection xml:id="sec-assessments-02">
    <title>About MTH 202</title>

    <p>
      <alert>MTH 202</alert> is our second semester course in
      calculus, and you will start to see some more authentic,
      real-world uses of calculus.  Some students have told me after
      this class, <q>Now I understand why people have told me I need
      to take math.</q>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>
        Catalog Description​ of MTH 202, Calculus II
      </title>
      <p>
        <em>
          MTH 202 is a continuation of MTH 201 [Calculus I] using
          graphical, numerical, and analytic methods to study
          exponential, logarithmic, hyperbolic, and inverse
          trigonometric functions. Indeterminate forms, improper
          integrals, integration techniques, sequences and series,
          Taylor polynomials, and power series. Offered every
          semester.
        </em>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <!--
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Course Content</title>
      <p>
        We will study all or nearly all of the material in chapters
        5-8 of our textbook, Active Calculus.  Your goal should be, as
        part of our ongoing work together, to read the text itself in
        full, and to complete all of the activities in the text.
        Preview Activities will normally be assigned for credit (based
        on effort and completeness) as part of your Daily Prep
        Assignments; the other activities in the text will be a
        central focus of our in-class discussions and work, and it’s
        your responsibility (with lots of support from your instructor
        and one another) to make sure you understand them and work
        through them fully, plus part of your semester grade will
        involve posting solutions and commenting on them.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    -->

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Prerequisites</title>
      <p>​ MTH 201 (Calculus I) or GV placement.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Learning Objectives</title>

      <p>
        After successful completion of this course, you will
        be able to:
        <ul>
          <li><p>
            Explain the First and Second Fundamental Theorem of
            Calculus, techniques and applications of integrals, series,
            particularly Taylor polynomials and series, and Differential
            Equations;
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Interpret the interrelationships between information
            given in graphical, numerical, and symbolic forms;
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Use communication skills, both verbal and written, in mathematics.;
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Apply the concepts of integration to solve mathematical
            problems in geometry, physics and economics;
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Apply quantitative and qualitative methods to differential
            equations to study applied problems such as population
            growth;
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Determine an appropriate method to calculate a given
            integral;
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            Use an appropriate test to determine convergence of a given
            series.
          </p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Assessment and how grades will be determined</title>

    <p>There will be several ways in which you will be assessed in
    this course and which will contribute to your final grade.

    <dl>
      <li><title>Daily prep 10%</title>
      <p>
        Before most class meetings, you will
        be asked to do some work to help prepare for our time together
        in class.  This will often involve working a few short
        problems and providing me with feedback on your preparation.
        These will be graded according to the effort you have put into
        them rather than correctness.
      </p>
      </li>
      <li><title>Homework on Runestone 15%</title>
      <p>
        Our book is hosted on Runestone Academy and contains a free
        online homework system that you will use regularly to complete
        assignments.  This will provide you with an opportunity for
        you to practice important skills required for success in this
        course and obtain immediate feedback on your work as
        well as the chance to attempt problems multiple times.
      </p>
      </li>
      <li><title>Learning targets 15%</title>
      <p>
        We will have weekly proficiency
        quizzes addressing a set of
        <xref ref="targets" text="custom">
        Proficiency Learning Targets
        </xref> that
        measure your understanding of certain essential skills you
        need to build a deep understanding of calculus.  Since
        this is an important part of the course, more details are
        provided later in this document.
      </p>
      </li>
      <!--
      <li><title>Written homework 20%</title>
      <p>
        You will have written homework assignments roughly every week.
        The assigned problems will ask you to use the skills assessed
        in the Proficiency Learning Targets as you solve multi-part,
        large-scale problems.  You will be expected to write carefully
        about your work, including justifications of any techniques
        you have used and a summary of your results and conclusions.
        Each problem will be graded on a four-point scale: Exemplary
        (“E”) is 4 points, Satisfactory (“S”) is 3, and Not Yet (“NY”)
        is 0.
      </p>
      </li>
      -->
      <li><title>Labs 10%</title>
      <p>About every other week, you’ll start a
        lab or activity in class that you’ll complete and write up
        outside of class for a formal grade; normally these will be
        done in groups of two and assigned a single group grade.
      </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Exams 30%</title>
        <p>
          There will be two in-class exams whose dates will be
          announced well in advance.  Exams will be conducted without
          notes or computers.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Final exam 20%</title>
        <p>
          The comprehensive final exam will consist of
          questions that address big-picture ideas in the course, and
          your work on those problems will constitute 20% of your
          semester grade.  Our final is on
          Thursday, December 17, 2:00 pm - 3:50 pm 
          in our usual classroom.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
    </p>

